00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.679 align:start position:0%
trate secrecy is high on the agenda this  month as European Union ministers  responsible for ensuring competitiveness  meet in Brussels The ministers will try  to agree a common approach to new rules  to better protect trade secrets which  are vital to businesses and underpinned  fair competition the aims to make it  easier for national courts to deal with  the problem and for victims to be  compensated as well as remove products  that infringe on trade secrets from the  market a council agreement would pave  the way for negotiations to begin with  the European Parliament The ministers  will also seek agreement on a  requirement for new cars to be fitted  with an emergency call system for use in  accidents throughout Europe known as  equal the council agreed in early May to  deploy the eu-wide system which would  consist of a wireless device that  manufacturers fit to cars and which  would trigger a call to the 112  emergency number it's hoped that equal  can speed up the emergency response to  road accidents and bring down casualty  numbers as the Union looks to half  traffic deaths by the year twenty twenty
 

